2026-11-04
- build: add the opt-in `--enable-hardening` and `--enable-sanitizers[=LIST]`
  configure switches. Hardening reports truncation, string-constant,
  ignored-result, null-dereference and format defects and adds stack protection
  and fortified libc calls; sanitizers build and link with
  `-fsanitize=address,undefined` by default. Ordinary builds are unchanged.
- docs: record the first hardening warning and cppcheck baseline and a
  per-component audit ledger, and add `scripts/warning-baseline.py` to compare
  a build's warning counts with that baseline.
- dtspcd, DtSvc: validate SPC requests before using them. The request buffer
  holds a full-size payload and its terminating NUL, and a payload above
  `SPC_BUFSIZ` ends the connection instead of overrunning the buffer. Headers
  must be fixed-width hexadecimal and name a defined request type; the
  dispatcher no longer indexes one entry past its table or calls a type with no
  handler; a request that needs a channel and names none is answered with an
  error. Counted-string and multi-packet readers work within the payload and
  reject malformed, short or oversized counts instead of reading stale buffer
  bytes or writing outside their vector. The termios, debug-file and
  application-spawn handlers check their fields and bound their buffers.
  Channel ids come from a counter, not the channel address.
- dtspcd: register a user fail-closed. The authentication file name has a random
  suffix, must be a regular file with a single link, and its owner must match
  the user named; the daemon verifies that it gave up root (groups, group,
  user, and that root cannot be regained) and exits if any step fails, and a
  home directory or shell that does not fit is refused. A failed `initgroups`
  is logged correctly instead of passing an integer to `%s`.
- dtspcd: add request-level regression tests (`make -C programs/dtspcd check`)
  that link the built `libDtSvc` and run under the sanitizers.
- DtSvc: fix a heap overflow in data-type lookup. The scratch buffer for
  database attribute text never grew past 100 bytes, so a name pattern, content
  test or type name of 100 bytes or more overflowed it, and attribute values of
  1024 bytes or more overflowed the buffer that expands them. Both buffers now
  grow to the value and report allocation failure. Content tests,
  `.DtDirDataType` parsing, `%dir%`/`%name%`/`%base%` expansion of names without
  a directory, backquote expansion (which now closes its pipe and no longer uses
  command text as a format), attribute and type-name lists (`DtDtsFindAttribute`
  overran its list when every type matched) and lookup state are bounded or
  checked as well. Sanitizer-built regression tests run under `make check` in
  `lib/DtSvc`.
- dtterm: when installed setuid root, give up root for good before starting the
  shell and stop if that does not take effect; a user at a process limit could
  previously get a shell with root privileges. Returning the window process to
  the user identity is checked the same way. A test with a fake kernel covers
  refused and ignored changes and a remaining way back.
- dtcm, libcsa: harden the calendar manager request path. Log records, entry
  tables and attribute lists are built in buffers that grow with the data,
  escaped, and read back by a lexer sized by the file; calendar names are
  copied without a fixed buffer, and an empty name or one containing '/' is
  refused; the operator array of lookup, archive and restore requests no longer
  overwrites the attribute count; attribute numbers sent by clients are
  resolved by name and bounded; use-after-free in calendar creation and
  old-format entry update, deletion of several appointments, re-creation of an
  existing calendar, and root privilege after a failed chmod or chown are
  fixed; the garbage-collection signals are held back while a request runs.
- dtcm: add request-level regression tests (`make -C programs/dtcm/server
  check`) that include the production log writer and request handlers and run
  under the sanitizers.
- dtlogin: harden XDMCP, authorization and signal handling. A Request offering
  only loopback or malformed addresses no longer indexes past its arrays,
  XDM-AUTHORIZATION-1 without the handshake is declined, and authorization
  cookies come from /dev/urandom instead of the clock. The Willing status, xrdb
  run, backup and server authority files no longer use temporary names,
  redirects or a shell. Signal handlers set flags and the main loop acts on
  them, failed privilege drops stop the server or session, and sanitizer tests
  run under `make check`.
- scripts/warning-baseline.py: compare a build with the assembled validation
  record as well as with a bare `record` output.
- dtterm: when installed setuid root, return to the user's identity as the first
  thing main() does. Until the first terminal widget existed, the toolkit read
  resource files and search paths named by the caller's environment, such as
  XENVIRONMENT, with the installed identity, so a user could have a file only
  root can read parsed as resources. A source guard checks the order.
- tt: harden ttsession's decoding of requests from clients that are not yet
  authenticated. Strings, byte strings and string lists with a negative or
  oversized length, session addresses with long host names, null objects and
  truncated bodies no longer crash the daemon or overrun buffers (one RPC call
  with a huge cookie length killed ttsession before). The session cookie comes
  from `/dev/urandom` only, and no session starts if it cannot be read.
- tt: add sanitizer tests for ttsession's decoding and request handling that
  run the production code (`make -C lib/tt/slib check`).
- tt: apply ttsession's authentication level to every request. At the unix and
  des levels a request with no credentials was served; it is now refused, as is
  a request with credentials too weak for the level. A failed secure-RPC
  credential lookup refuses the request, ttsession refuses `-a des` in a build
  without secure RPC, the cookie is compared without stopping at the first
  difference, and the unix and none levels log a warning when ttsession starts.
- tt: limit the environment variables a message can set for a started ptype. The
  variables ttsession sets itself win over those of a message, names that change
  which code a program runs, whom it believes it is or how it finds its session
  are not passed on, and `TOOLTALK_START_ENV` lets the owner of the session
  approve names or restrict the set to a list. A variable that cannot be set
  stops the start.
- tt: harden rpc.ttdbserverd against malformed requests. Descriptors outside
  the table of open files are refused, a NetISAM descriptor beyond the table
  is closed (an open that finds nothing to close fails instead of looping), and
  a failed `isopen` or `isbuild` no longer keeps a descriptor. A transaction
  with a stale descriptor no longer closes a server descriptor or removes an
  earlier log, and the log is opened without following links with its record
  lengths checked. Records from clients are copied into zero-padded buffers;
  missing key descriptors, short object keys, oversized session ids, empty or
  table-less paths, and message info or cache levels stored as properties are
  checked; `iscntl` replies from writable storage; `TT_GET_ALL_SESSIONS` no
  longer takes a NetISAM position from the wire; transaction record lists are
  decoded without recursion; requests that fail to decode are freed.
- tt: keep the record lengths that `_Tt_isam_file` and `_Tt_client_isam_file`
  read from the file (they were reset to zero afterwards, so every record
  buffer held one byte), and move overlapping b-tree entries in mini_isam with
  `memmove`.
- tt: add sanitizer tests that run the real dispatcher of rpc.ttdbserverd on
  made-up requests (`make -C lib/tt/bin/ttdbserverd check`).
- tt: make NetISAM and its users agree on the width of a long. On Linux the
  feature macros of the build hide `LONG_BIT` from glibc's `<limits.h>` in C
  code while the C++ compilers show it, and the headers fell back to 32, so on
  a 64-bit build `libisam` stored 4-byte longs where rpc.ttdbserverd and libtt
  sized keys and records for 8-byte ones: the daemon could not build the tables
  of a partition (it ended in the second index of the first table), and a
  stored -1 came back as 4294967295. `isam.h`, `tt_client_isam.h`,
  `tt_db_server_consts.h` and `tt_old_db_consts.h` now take the fallback from
  `ULONG_MAX`, so 32-bit builds are unchanged and 64-bit builds use 8-byte
  longs in C and in C++. `stfloat` stores a float at its own width, not at the
  width of a long. `make -C lib/tt/mini_isam check` runs the check as C and as
  C++ against `libisam`, and the partition cases of the rpc.ttdbserverd tests
  no longer skip.

